
```text
toast-rust-dashboard/
├── backend/
└── frontend/
```

run **each project in its own terminal**.

## 1. Start the Rust backend

Open a terminal:

```bash
cd backend
```

Make sure you're inside the folder:

```bash
pwd
```

You should see something like:

```text
.../toast-rust-dashboard/backend
```

Then run:

```bash
cargo run
```

If everything is set up correctly, you'll see something similar to:

```text
Compiling backend v0.1.0
Finished dev [unoptimized + debuginfo]
Running `target/debug/backend`
```

If you're using **Actix-web**, you'll eventually see something like:

```text
Server running at http://127.0.0.1:8080
```

---

## 2. Start the React frontend

Open **another terminal**.

```bash
cd frontend
npm install
npm run dev
```

Vite will usually start on:

```text
http://localhost:5173
```

Now you'll have

```
React
localhost:5173
      │
      │ HTTP
      ▼
Rust Actix
localhost:8080
```

---

## 3. If `cargo run` fails...

Run:

```bash
ls
```

You should see:

```text
Cargo.toml
src
```

If you **don't** see `Cargo.toml`, you're not inside the Rust project.

---

## 4. If you haven't written the server yet

`cargo new backend` only creates a basic Rust console app.

Your `src/main.rs` will probably look like:

```rust
fn main() {
    println!("Hello, world!");
}
```

Running

```bash
cargo run
```

will simply print:

```text
Hello, world!
```

That's normal.

To make it a web server, we'll add **Actix-web** next. Go to rust-actix-web setup.

